Title: The Goophered Grapevine
Credit: Adapted from the tale by Charles W. Chesnutt (1899)
Order: 1
Production Code: 1x01

> FADE IN:

EXT. THE VINEYARD - DUSK

AMB: crickets, the dry shiver of wind through unpruned vines

A pine log under a spreading elm. Rotting rail fence, a straight sandy lane, the standing chimneys of a house the war destroyed. Wild scuppernong vines have swallowed the trellises.

Uncle Julius McAdoo sits on the log with a hat full of grapes on his knee and a pile of skins beside him. He looks directly into the lens as if he has been waiting for us.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
Evenin'. Set down, ef you'd lack. Dey's room on de log.

He eats a grape. Takes his time.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Some folks'll tell you a vimya'd is des dirt en roots en sun. Dat's w'at dey tells you. But I's be'n on dis same groun' sence befo' de wah, en I's here ter tell you de groun' got a mem'ry. En it doan fergit who owes it w'at.

MUSIC: BED IN - low, patient, unresolved

JULIUS (CONT'D)
I doan ax you ter b'lieve it. I des ax you ter set a minute.

He spits a seed into the dark. Wind through the vines, almost a word.

SUPER: "THE CONJURE WOMAN"

SFX: a bottle, somewhere, rolling on wood

EXT. SANDY LANE - DAY

Earlier, in ordinary daylight. A buggy picks its way up the sand between the decayed gateposts. John, in a Northern coat, drives; Annie, pale and straight-backed, holds a parasol.

SUPER: "NORTH CAROLINA. SOME YEARS AFTER THE WAR."

JOHN
Five miles from town, and I don't think we've met a soul.

ANNIE
That's the soul. The whole county is resting.

JOHN
(smiling)
It's not too late to look at a farm in France.

ANNIE
John. The doctor said warm, and quiet. This is both.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - CONTINUOUS

John helps Annie down. They look at the chimneys, the vines, the sky. Annie is tired; John steers her toward the log under the elm.

One end of it is occupied. Julius rises politely, hat of grapes in hand, and starts to move off.

JOHN (CONT'D)
Don't let us disturb you. There's plenty of room for us all.

Julius sits again, with some embarrassment, and goes on eating, a little less happily. John studies him: tall, a little bowed, bald on top, bushy-haired at the sides, quick-eyed.

JOHN (CONT'D)
Do you live around here?

JULIUS
Yas, suh. I lives des ober yander, behine de nex' san'-hill, on de Lumberton plank-road.

JOHN
Do you know anything about when this vineyard was worked?

JULIUS
Lawd bless you, suh, I knows all about it. Dey ain' na'er a man in dis settlement w'at won' tell you ole Julius McAdoo 'uz bawn en raise' on dis yer same plantation.
(a look at them both)
Is you de Norv'n gemman w'at's gwine ter buy de ole vimya'd?

JOHN
I'm looking at it. I don't know that I'll buy unless I can be reasonably sure of making something out of it.

JULIUS
Well, suh, you is a stranger ter me, en I is a stranger ter you, en we is bofe strangers ter one anudder, but 'f I 'uz in yo' place, I wouldn' buy dis vimya'd.

JOHN
Why not?

JULIUS
I dunno whe'r you b'lieves in cunj'in' er not,--some er de w'ite folks don't, er says dey don't,--but de truf er de matter is dat dis yer ole vimya'd is goophered.

JOHN
Is what?

JULIUS
Is goophered. Cunju'd. Bewitch'.

Annie moves closer to John. Julius notices, and is solemn about it.

JOHN
How do you know it's bewitched?

JULIUS
I wouldn' spec' fer you ter b'lieve me 'less you know all 'bout de fac's. But ef you en young miss dere doan min' lis'nin' ter a ole nigger run on a minute er two w'ile you er restin', I kin 'splain ter you how it all happen'.

ANNIE
(low)
John. Please.

JOHN
We would be glad to hear how it all happened.

Julius looks past them, at something that isn't there. His voice slows and softens.

JULIUS
Ole Mars Dugal' McAdoo bought dis place long many years befo' de wah...

MUSIC: BED OUT

SUPER: "BEFORE THE WAR."

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - DAY (BEFORE THE WAR)

The same hillside, but with its house standing. White-painted, wide-porched. Rows of scuppernong vines trained on arbors, green and heavy, shining.

JULIUS (V.O.)
He sot out all dis yer part in scuppernon's, en made a thousan' gallon er wine eve'y year. Now, ef dey's an'thing a nigger lub, nex' ter 'possum, en chick'n, en watermillyums, it's scuppernon's.

A line of enslaved men and women in the field. A boy watching the grapes the way a boy watches a loaf.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
So atter a w'ile Mars Dugal' begin ter miss his scuppernon's.

Mars Dugal' McAdoo, broad and brash, in a planter's coat, stands before the arbor counting empty stems with the overseer. His face is red.

MARS DUGAL'
Count 'em again.

OVERSEER
Dey's thirty vimes stripped, Mars Dugal'.

MARS DUGAL'
Steel traps. Spring guns. Sit up nights, you and me.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Mars Dugal' sot spring guns en steel traps, tel one night Mars Dugal'--he 'uz a monst'us keerless man--got his own leg shot full er cow-peas.

SFX: a flat boom, a bellow, a dog barking

Mars Dugal' on the ground clutching his leg while the overseer stares. In the quarters, a hundred yards off, a lamp is turned down very quietly.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Dey wuz a cunjuh 'oman livin' down 'mongs' de free niggers on de Wim'l'ton Road, en all de darkies fum Rockfish ter Beaver Crick wuz feared er her.

EXT. THE FREE SETTLEMENT - DAY

A low cabin among pines. Gourds and bottles on a shelf behind a doorway no one can see into. A tall, still woman in a head-handkerchief.

SFX: a hornet's drone, brief

Aun' Peggy stands in the door with arms folded. Mars Dugal' stands on the path with a covered basket: chicken, pound-cake, a bottle of scuppernong wine.

MARS DUGAL'
My wife sent this.

AUN' PEGGY
Yo' wife is a kind lady.

MARS DUGAL'
I want a word with you about my grapes.

She steps aside to let him in. The door closes. Through the crack, only a hand, and a basket being taken.

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - VINEYARD - DAY

Aun' Peggy walks the arbors unhurried. The enslaved watch her from the cotton field and slow down. She takes a leaf here, a grape-hull there, a seed, a twig, a pinch of dirt. In a big black bottle: a snake's tooth, a speckled hen's gall, hairs from a black cat's tail, scuppernong wine.

JULIUS (V.O.)
She buried it under de root uv a red oak tree, en tole one er de niggers a'er a nigger w'at eat dem grapes 'ud be sho' ter die inside'n twel' mont's.

MUSIC: STING - the bottle goes into the ground

Aun' Peggy stamps the dirt flat. Looks directly at the quarter. They all look away.

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - SMOKEHOUSE - DAY

A strange gentleman's coachman, a long way from home, slips behind the smokehouse and eats every grape he can hold. A moment later, a carriage rattles away down the lane.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dat night, on de way home, de gemman's hoss runned away en kill' de coachman.

SFX: hoofbeats, a scream, then nothing

AUN' LUCY, the cook, at the kitchen door, says nothing and goes back inside.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Den one er de nigger chilluns runned away fum de quarters, en got in de scuppernon's, en died de nex' week. W'ite folks say he die' er de fevuh. But de niggers knowed it wuz de goopher.

The vines hang heavy and untouched. Mars Dugal' on the porch, laughing, with the overseer.

MARS DUGAL'
Fifteen hundred gallon on ten dollars laid out. Dat's what I call good intrus'!

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - QUARTERS - DAY

Henry, older, the color of gingerbread, bald as a horse-apple, steps down from Mars Dugal's wagon with a bundle. He is peart for his years, and the quarter looks him over.

JULIUS (V.O.)
So de goopher did n' wuk no mo' tel de nex' summer, when one er de fiel' han's died, en Mars Dugal' went ter town fer ter buy anudder.

That night, in the dark, Henry is alone by the arbor with a handful of grapes and the sweet smell of the vines.

Next morning, in the quarter, Henry tells the others about the "fine bait er scuppernon'" he ate. They stop and stare. He sees their faces. He goes the color of old ash.

HENRY
Whut?

In the overseer's office, the overseer pours a drink and hands it to him.

OVERSEER
Nex' rainy day I take you ober ter Aun' Peggy's.

INT. AUN' PEGGY'S CABIN - DAY

Rain on a plank roof. Aun' Peggy pours something dark from a bottle into a gourd.

AUN' PEGGY
Seein' ez he did n' know, I reckon I mought take it off'n him.

HENRY
Thank you, ma'am.

She holds the gourd out and does not let go.

AUN' PEGGY
It'll keep de goopher off'n you tel de spring. But w'en de sap begin ter rise, you come back, en I tell you w'at ter do.

SFX: a gourd rattles on a shelf

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - VINEYARD - DAY (SPRING)

Henry, with a ham under one arm, comes to the cabin; later he scrapes sap from a cut vine, rubs it onto his bald head. Cut to him kneeling by a trellis, smiling. Little curls begin to rise on his skull like a bunch of grapes.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Aun' Peggy tole 'im, w'en Mars Dugal' begin ter prune de grapevimes, he mus' go en scrape off de sap, en 'n'int his ball head wid it. En bein' ez he fotch her de ham, she fix' it so he kin eat all de scuppernon' he want.

Montage as the year turns:

Spring: the leaves come, Henry's hair comes. By summer he has the biggest head of hair on the plantation, curled tight into little balls. He dances with a girl in the quarter.

SFX: a fiddle, a bright laugh

Fall: the grapes are gathered; the knots straighten out; the leaves fall; his hair falls out; he is bent, bald, stiff.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Now, ef you 'd 'a' knowed ole Mars Dugal' McAdoo, you 'd 'a' knowed dat it ha' ter be a mighty rainy day when he could n' fine sump'n fer his niggers ter do.

EXT. PATESVILLE - MAIN STREET - DAY

Mars Dugal' leads a young, springy Henry down the street to a buyer.

JULIUS (V.O.)
He tuk Henry ter town, en sole 'im fer fifteen hunder' dollars.

Then, a winter day, Mars Dugal' and the man who bought him, smoking on the porch of a store.

MARS DUGAL'
How you like de nigger I sole you las' spring?

BUYER
Spec' I made a bad bahgin. Henry done good wuk all de summer, but sence de fall set in he 'pears ter be sorter pinin' away. His ha'r is all fell out, en ef he don't pick up his strenk mighty soon, I spec' I'm gwine ter lose 'im.

MARS DUGAL'
Well, a bahgin 's a bahgin, but you en me is good fren's. Ef he gits any wusser I'll be willin' ter gib yer five hund'ed dollars fer 'im, en take my chances on his livin'.

Mars Dugal' lights his cigar. Behind him, in the dim of the store window, a calendar on the wall, winter still deep.

JULIUS (V.O.)
En he kep' dat sellin' business up fer five year er mo'. Mars Dugal' made 'nuff money off'n Henry ter buy anudder plantation ober on Beaver Crick.

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - BIG HOUSE - DAY

A stranger in a Northern suit steps down from a buggy with a long case of drawings and a new winepress on a wagon behind him: the The Yankee Vintner. He and Mars Dugal' shake hands, long and warm.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Den one day dey come a Yankee ter stop at de plantation. He promus Mars Dugal' he c'd make de grapevimes b'ar twice't ez many grapes.

AMB: shovels in dirt, a long afternoon

Men dig under the roots of every scuppernong vine. The earth lies open for a week. The Yankee pours a mixture of lime and ashes and manure around the roots, then trims every vine back to the stub. All the while, he eats at the big house, plays cards at night with Mars Dugal'.

INT. THE McADOO BIG HOUSE - NIGHT

Cards. The Yankee is dealing. Mars Dugal' is laughing. The lamp flickers.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dey say Mars Dugal' los' mo'n a thousan' dollars dyoin' er de week dat Yankee wuz a-ruinin' de grapevimes.

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - VINEYARD - DAY (SUMMER)

The leaves shrivel and curl. The young grapes turn yellow. Mars Dugal' waters the vines with his own hands, in his shirt-sleeves, in the heat.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dat Yankee done dug too close under de roots, en prune de branches too close ter de vime, en all dat lime en ashes done burn' de life out'n de vimes.

INT. HENRY'S CABIN - DAY

Henry in bed, thin, rheumatic, still. Through the window, the big grapevine halfway between the quarters and the house, withered and yellow.

JULIUS (V.O.)
When de big vime whar he got de sap ter 'n'int his head withered en turned yaller en died, Henry died too. Des went out, sorter like a cannel.

Henry's breath stops. A candle in the window gutters, goes out.

MUSIC: STING - the flame

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - DAY

Mars Dugal' stands in the stripped vineyard in his shirt-sleeves, red-faced. Fury, grief, a little disbelief.

JULIUS (V.O.)
He swo' dat ef he could git holt er dat Yankee he 'd wear 'im ter a frazzle, en den chaw up de frazzle.

SFX: a drum, distant, a regiment forming

Mars Dugal' in a uniform coat, at the head of a company, mounted, riding out of the lane. Behind him the house, already a little shabby.

JULIUS (V.O.)
W'en de wah broke out, Mars Dugal' raise' a comp'ny, en went off ter fight de Yankees. Atter de s'render ole miss move' ter town, de niggers all scattered 'way, en de vimya'd ain' be'n cultervated sence.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - DUSK

Back at the log. Julius, quiet. John and Annie sit, quiet. A cricket starts again.

ANNIE
(doubtfully, but seriously)
Is that story true?

JULIUS
It's des ez true ez I'm a-settin' here, miss. Dey's a easy way ter prove it: I kin lead de way right ter Henry's grave ober yander in de plantation buryin'-groun'.

He waits. Nobody asks for the grave.

JOHN
But I thought you said all the old vines died.

JULIUS
Dey did 'pear ter die, but a few un 'em come out ag'in, en is mixed in 'mongs' de yuthers. I ain' skeered ter eat de grapes, 'caze I knows de old vimes fum de noo ones; but wid strangers dey ain' no tellin' w'at mought happen. I would n' 'vise yer ter buy dis vimya'd.

John looks at the vines, at his wife, at the sky. He is, deep down, a man who buys things.

SUPER: "SIX MONTHS LATER."

EXT. THE VINEYARD - DAY

Sun, trellises mended, vines pruned, crates stacked. A sign on the gatepost, new paint: The Vineyard and a Northern shipping mark.

John, in his shirt-sleeves, walks the rows with a ledger. Annie, pinker, watches from the porch of a new house.

Behind a row of vines, Julius, in a coachman's coat, is eating grapes out of his hat. A cabin door stands open behind him. A bushel basket of grapes on the step, with a handwritten card.

John comes up on him from behind and stops.

JOHN
Julius.

JULIUS
(smooth)
Marnin', suh.

JOHN
I seem to remember you advising me not to buy this place.

JULIUS
Yas, suh. I did.

JOHN
And you seem to have been getting a little revenue off these vines for a number of years.

JULIUS
Co'se, suh, I wuz doin' it fer de sake er de vimes. Dey needs a man ter watch 'em.

JOHN
Do you think any of the old goopher is left?

JULIUS
(solemn)
I ain' seed no signs, suh. But I's heared tell dey's folks roun' heah w'at doan suffer fer want er scuppernon's w'en de season come.

A very long pause. John looks at the basket, then at Julius, and at the new coachman's coat.

JOHN
(dry)
I believe I paid you wages for driving, did I?

JULIUS
Yas, suh. En mighty good wages. I's ain' complainin'.

JOHN
They were meant to be more than a fair exchange for any income you lost.

JULIUS
Dey is, suh. Dey is.

Julius offers him a grape from the hat. John, after a moment, takes it. Eats. His eyebrows go up.

JOHN
(despite himself)
That is a very good grape.

JULIUS
Sugar ain' a suckumstance ter scuppernon', suh.

MUSIC: BED IN - low, wry

EXT. THE VINEYARD - DUSK

Julius, alone again on the log, hat of grapes, looking straight at us. The vines behind him are full of fruit.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
Now you knows w'at I knows. Henry died, en Mars Dugal' fergot, en de vines come back. Whuther de goopher's still in de groun', dat's fer you ter say. My bizness is de grapes.

He puts one in his mouth. A long, sweet pause.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Come back nex' week. I's got a tale 'bout a schoolhouse. Dey's sump'n in dat lumber dat won' stop talkin'.

He smiles. Wind in the vines.

MUSIC: STING - a single cello note, unresolved

> FADE OUT.

THE END
